<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salah Rais</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1488–<a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Salih Reis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salah Rais (c. 1488–1556) was an Ottoman corsair and admiral who served as beylerbey of the Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> from 1552 to 1556. A veteran of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a>'s campaigns, he extended Algerian power into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1555. He died of plague in 1556 while preparing an attack on Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Salah Rais was born either in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in Ottoman Egypt or at Kazdağ near Çanakkale, and his origin is given variously as Turkish, Egyptian, Arab or Moorish. European sources call him Sala Reis, Salek Rais or Cale Arraez. Very young, he joined the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oruc-reis">Oruç Reis</a>, the elder of the Barbarossa brothers, who operated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary Coast</a>, and he became one of their chief lieutenants. After Oruç was killed fighting the Spaniards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1518, Salah served under his brother Hızır Reis, and in 1520 he went with him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Turgut Reis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a> and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Bône</a>, then under Spanish control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1529 he raided the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> with 14 galliots and then joined Aydın Reis near Formentera, where the Spanish fleet was destroyed and its commander Rodrigo de Portuondo killed; Salah took Portuondo's son prisoner. He sailed with Barbarossa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1532 and was among the captains received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>. In July 1535 he defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, drawing the forces of Girolamo Tuttavilla, Count of Sarno, into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> in which Tuttavilla was killed.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-preveza">Battle of Preveza</a> in September 1538 he commanded the 24 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> of the Ottoman right wing, and his men boarded the great Venetian flagship. In 1539 he helped retake Castelnuovo from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and in October 1543, after the Franco-Ottoman capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, he sacked Rosas, Palamós and other towns of the Costa Brava in Catalonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> from the Knights of St. John in 1551, where he bombarded their fortress until its commander surrendered, he was made beylerbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and admiral of the Ottoman western fleet. He reached Algiers in April 1552. That year his troops marched into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and took the oasis town of Touggourt, and Ottoman forces recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>. In 1555, with 40,000 men, he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bougie</a>; after 14 days of bombardment its governor Alfonso di Peralta made terms. The defenders were allowed to leave, yet about 400 men, 120 women and 100 children were captured and enslaved by corsairs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salah Rais” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Algerian forces took Bejaia from Spain in 1555 under <b>Salah Rais</b>, ended the Zayyanid kingdom of Tlemcen in the 1550s and besieged Oran and Mers el-Kébir in 1563 without success.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1555 <b>Salah Rais</b>, beylerbey of the Regency of Algiers, besieged and took Bejaia.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-bougie">Spanish Bougie</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Biga, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.23,27.24&amp;year=1532" aria-label="Biga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Salah Rais"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4820 4128h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4820 4128h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.23,27.24&amp;year=1532">What was here in 1532? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
